

🚫 IBM DFDL Unsupported Features (v10.1.0.6)

Note:
IBM Integration Bus / ACE ne DFDL 1.0 specification ka full implementation nahi kiya.
Kuch features, properties, functions, aur annotations IBM me supported nahi hain.
Yeh features mostly rare use cases ke liye hote hain, jaise bidirectional text, stop values, ya complex calendar/timezone handling.


---

🔹 1. Unsupported Property Enumerations

dfdl:textBiDi="yes" → Bidirectional text (Arabic/Hebrew) not supported.

dfdl:occursCountKind="stopValue" → Arrays terminated by stop value not supported.

dfdl:binaryCalendarRep="packed" → Packed binary calendar representation not supported.

dfdl:floating="yes" → Floating elements not supported.

dfdl:lengthKind="endOfParent" → Element ending at parent’s end not supported.

dfdl:assert testKind="pattern" → Pattern-based assertions not supported.

dfdl:discriminator testKind="pattern" → Pattern-based discriminators not supported.

dfdl:separatorSuppressionPolicy="trailingEmptyStrict" → Not supported.

dfdl:encodingErrorPolicy="replace" → Encoding error policy “replace” not supported.

dfdl:textZonedSignStyle="asciiTandemModified" → Not supported.

dfdl:bitOrder="leastSignificantBitFirst" → LSB first bit order not supported.

dfdl:escapeCharacterPolicy="delimiters" → Not supported.



---

🔹 2. Unsupported Properties

dfdl:hiddenGroupRef

dfdl:inputValueCalc

dfdl:outputValueCalc


👉 Runtime calculated values or hidden groups are not supported.


---

🔹 3. Unsupported Functions (DFDL Expression Language)

fn:count

fn:exactly-one

fn:name

fn:local-name

fn:namespace-uri

dfdl:contentLength

dfdl:valueLength

dfdl:testBit, dfdl:setBits

dfdl:occursIndex

dfdl:checkConstraints

dfdl:timeZoneFromDate, dfdl:timeZoneFromDateTime, dfdl:timeZoneFromTime

All DFDL constructor functions

dfdl:decodeDFDLentities, dfdl:encodeDFDLentities, dfdl:containsDFDLentities


⚠️ IBM DFDL supports only basic XPath and arithmetic functions — advanced ones not supported.


---

🔹 4. Unsupported Annotations

dfdl:defineVariable with external="true"

dfdl:newVariableInstance

dfdl:assert on global element/simple type

dfdl:discriminator on global element/simple type


👉 External or dynamic variables, and pattern-based assertions/discriminators are restricted.


---

🔹 5. Other Restrictions and Behavioral Differences

Default values not supported during parsing.

During serialization, if element is a choice, only first branch default used.

dfdl:lengthKind="implicit" may end early if higher-level delimiter found.

BOM (Byte Order Mark) is not auto-detected, must be explicitly modeled.

Only one xs:appinfo per xs:annotation allowed.

dfdl:lengthKind="prefixed" cannot reference a type that also has prefixed.

dfdl:lengthKind="pattern" not allowed with binary data.

Missing/empty/absent element distinction not as per spec.

Nil values cannot include DFDL character entities for binary elements.

Decimal & calendar restrictions:

dfdl:textStandardDecimalSeparator cannot be a list.

dfdl:textStandardExponentRep must be one character.

@ not allowed in dfdl:textNumberPattern.

_ not allowed in dfdl:calendarLanguage.

dfdl:calendarTimeZone cannot be empty or Olson format.


Packed binary numbers must have length and alignment multiple of 4 bits.

All text elements align on 8-bit boundary (no non-8-bit encodings).

dfdl:assert and dfdl:discriminator messages cannot be expressions.

Path validation in expressions is incomplete (avoid ambiguous array refs).

%WSP*; entity classes incorrectly allowed; use %WSP+; instead.

dfdl:binaryBooleanTrueRep cannot be empty string.

fn:concat limited to four arguments.

Escape scheme applied after empty value delimiter policy during serialization.



---

💡 Summary for Developers

✅ IBM DFDL supports common industrial formats: CSV, fixed-length, COBOL copybook, ISO8583.
🚫 Avoid rare constructs like stopValue, BiDi text, packed calendars, or pattern-based checks.
⚙️ Always test schema deployment — some features may compile but fail at runtime.


---

Short Recap:

> DFDL Spec = Full buffet 🍱
IBM DFDL = Buffet ka practical subset (sirf industry-use dishes 🥗)



