⚙️ IBM DFDL Implementation-Specific Limits

Note:
DFDL specification ek open standard hai, par IBM Integration Bus / ACE ne apne parser ke performance aur stability ke liye kuch fixed limits rakhe hain.
Agar data in limits se bada hua — parsing ya serialization fail ho jayegi.

Unsupported Features → “Feature hi nahi bana IBM me.”
🔹 Implementation Limits → “Feature bana hai, par limit ke andar hi chalega.”

---

🔹 1. Maximum Digits in a Number → 1000 Digits

IBM parser ek single numeric value me maximum 1000 digits tak allow karta hai.
Agar number 1001 digits ka hai → error milega.

Example:
✅ 1234567890 → 10 digits (OK)
✅ 999... (1000 digits) → OK
❌ 111... (1001 digits) → Parser error


---

🔹 2. Maximum Numeric Precision → 33 Digits

Decimal ya integer numbers me precision (significant digits) 33 tak limited hai.
Agar 34+ digits huye, IBM undefined behavior dikha sakta hai (error/truncate/crash).

Example:
✅ 123456789012345678901234567890123 → 33 digits (OK)
❌ 12345678901234567890123456789012345 → 35 digits (Not supported)


---

🔹 3. Maximum Array Elements → 2,147,483,647 (≈ 2.1 Billion)

Agar tu maxOccurs="unbounded" use kare, IBM maximum 2.1 billion elements tak handle karega.
Usse zyada → parser error ya out-of-memory.

Example:
✅ 1 billion records → parse hoga (slow but OK)
❌ 3 billion records → error (limit exceed)


---

🔹 4. Fractional Seconds → Up to Milliseconds (3 digits)

IBM DFDL sirf milliseconds (3 decimal places) tak fractional seconds samajhta hai.
Microseconds (.123456) ya nanoseconds ignore ho jate hain.

Example:
✅ 2025-09-12T10:23:45.123 → OK
❌ 2025-09-12T10:23:45.123456 → IBM ignore karega .456


---

💡 Why These Limits Exist

Parser ko overflow ya crash se bachane ke liye

Performance aur memory efficiency maintain karne ke liye

DFDL standard ke practical subset ko follow karne ke liye



